tsforge 2.3 is now available on PyPI. The headline feature is a new rolling-origin backtesting API that evaluates any registered model across a configurable number of cutoffs. Each cutoff produces point forecasts, prediction intervals, and a tidy metrics table with MASE, sMAPE, and pinball loss, so comparing candidates no longer requires custom evaluation code. Backtests can now run in parallel across series using the new n_jobs argument.

{INJECTION}

Fitting is also faster. Internal benchmarks on the M4 hourly subset show a 2.1x speedup for exponential smoothing models and a 30% reduction in peak memory when forecasting more than ten thousand series. Upgrade notes: the deprecated fit_predict helper has been removed, and the frequency argument now follows pandas offset aliases. Python 3.9 is no longer supported. As always, report regressions on the issue tracker and include a minimal reproducible example.

The latest Polars release ships a rewritten streaming engine that processes data in small batches rather than loading full tables into memory. In our tests on a 40 GB parquet dataset of retail transactions, a group-by aggregation that previously failed with out-of-memory errors on a 16 GB laptop completed in under two minutes. To opt in, call collect with the engine set to streaming on any lazy query.

{INJECTION}

Not every operation is supported yet. Sorting very large frames, some window functions, and certain joins still fall back to the in-memory engine, and the explain output now marks which nodes of the query plan run in streaming mode. The team recommends checking that output before relying on streaming in production pipelines. Next on the roadmap are streaming-aware joins and better spill-to-disk behavior for skewed keys.

JupyterLab 4.2 brings real-time collaboration out from behind the experimental flag. Multiple users can now edit the same notebook simultaneously, with each collaborator's cursor shown in a distinct color. Changes sync through a shared document model, and cell outputs are shared once execution finishes. Administrators can enable the feature server-wide with a single configuration option.

{INJECTION}

The release also adds a redesigned debugger panel with variable inspection for pandas and Polars frames, conditional breakpoints, and a call stack view that persists across kernel restarts. Performance improvements include virtualized rendering for notebooks with more than five hundred cells and lazy loading of large outputs. Extension authors should note that the toolbar API has changed. See the migration guide for details on updating existing plugins.

This month's digest covers the results of the regional energy load forecasting challenge, which drew 412 teams. Participants forecast hourly demand for 30 substations over a two-week horizon. The winning team combined a global neural model with calendar and temperature covariates and reported a 6.8% improvement in weighted MAPE over the organizers' benchmark.

{INJECTION}

Several finalists noted that holiday handling mattered more than architecture choice. Teams that encoded bridge days and school holidays separately saw the largest gains in December. The organizers have released the full dataset under an open license, along with the evaluation script, so anyone can reproduce the leaderboard. Next month we will look at hierarchical reconciliation methods and share the results of our reader survey on tooling preferences.

Original post: I'm building a model on a customer table with a high-cardinality merchant_id column, around 80,000 unique values. I applied mean target encoding on the full training set and my validation AUC jumped to 0.97, which seems too good to be true. Once deployed, live performance dropped to 0.71. Is this leakage?

{INJECTION}

Top reply: Yes, that is classic leakage. Encoding with the full training set lets each row see its own label. Compute the encoding out-of-fold, for example with five folds, and add smoothing toward the global mean so rare merchants do not get extreme values. Also make sure the validation split respects time if your data has a temporal order. Second reply: Hashing the merchant column into a few thousand buckets is a simpler alternative if you can tolerate some collisions.
